g 9 points. There remain 6 points uncovered: which are (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Let's list all points in T_5 again: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3); (4,1),(4,2); (5,1). The three lines we have cover: y=x: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). y=-2x+7: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). x+2y=7: (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). So covered points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),(1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's 9 points. Uncovered: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 6 points.\n\nWe have 2 remaining sunny lines to cover these 6 points. Each sunny line can cover at most floor((k+1)/2) = floor(6/2) = 3 points. So each of the remaining lines must cover exactly 3 points, and they must be disjoint from each other and from the previous lines. So we need to find two sunny lines each covering 3 of the remaining points, covering all 6. Let's see if possible.\n\nRemaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Let's see if any sunny line can contain three of these points.\n\nWe need a line not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. Let's try slope 1: y = x + c. For points (1,2): c=1. Then line y = x+1 includes (1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5)... but (2,3) is not in remaining set (it's covered already). Also (3,4) not in T_5 (since 3+4=7 >6). So line y=x+1 contains only (1,2) among remaining points. So not enough.\n\nSlope -2: y = -2x + b. For (1,2): b = 4. Then line y = -2x + 4 includes (1,2),(2,0) (invalid), (0,4) (invalid). So only (1,2). Not enough.\n\nSlope -1/2: x + 2y = C. For (1,2): C = 1 + 4 = 5. Then line x+2y=5 includes (1,2),(3,1) (already covered), (5,0) invalid. So only (1,2) among remaining.\n\nTry slope 2: y = 2x + c. For (1,2): c = 0. Then line y = 2x includes (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) (invalid). So line y=2x contains (1,2) and (2,4) which are both in remaining set. That's 2 points. Also (3,6) not in T_5. So line y=2x covers two remaining points.\n\nTry slope 1/2: y = x/2 + c. For (2,1): c = 0. Then line y = x/2 includes (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) (invalid). So line y = x/2 covers (2,1) and (4,2) both in remaining set. That's 2 points.\n\nTry slope -3: y = -3x + b. For (1,2): b = 5. Then line y = -3x +5 includes (1,2),(2,-1) invalid. So only (1,2).\n\nTry slope 3: y = 3x + c. For (1,2): c = -1. Then line y = 3x -1 includes (1,2),(2,5) (2+5=7 >6 invalid). So only (1,2).\n\nTry slope -1/3: y = -x/3 + c. For (1,2): c = 2 + 1/3 = 7/3 not integer, but line can have rational intercept; but we need integer points. For integer points, we need x divisible by 3? Actually line y = -x/3 + c => 3y = -x + 3c => x + 3y = 3c. So integer points require x + 3y divisible by 3. For (1,2): 1+6=7 not divisible by 3, so not on integer line of that form. So not relevant.\n\nThus seems unlikely to cover remaining 6 points with two sunny lines each covering 3 points. Let's try to find any line that contains three of the remaining points. The remaining points are arranged in two columns: column x=1 has (1,2),(1,4); column x=2 has (2,1),(2,4); column x=4 has (4,1),(4,2). So we need a line that goes through three of these points. Let's examine possible triples.\n\nTriples could be (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) but (3,0) not in T_5. (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not in T_5. (1,4),(2,1),(3,-2) invalid. (1,2),(4,1),(7,0) invalid. (1,4),(4,2),(7,0) invalid. (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) invalid. (2,4),(4,2),(6,0) invalid. So no triple of remaining points lies on a line with integer slope? Let's check systematically.\n\nWe need three points (x1,y1),(x2,y2),(x3,y3) among remaining points that are collinear. Compute slopes between pairs:\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,1) = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1. That's slope -1, which is forbidden (non-sunny). So can't use that line.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,4) = (4-2)/(2-1) = 2. So line slope 2 passes through (1,2) and (2,4). Does it also pass through any other remaining point? Check (3,6) not in T_5. (4,8) not in T_5. So only those two.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (4,1) = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3. So line slope -1/3 passes through (1,2) and (4,1). Does it also pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(2-1) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 not integer. So no other integer point.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (4,2) = (2-2)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal line, forbidden.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,1) we already did.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,4) we did.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,1) is -1 (forbidden). So any line containing (1,2) and (2,1) is not sunny.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (2,4) is 2 (sunny). So line y=2x? Actually line through (1,2) slope 2: y-2 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x. Indeed passes through (2,4). So line y=2x covers (1,2),(2,4). It also passes through (3,6) not in T_5. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (4,1) is -1/3 (sunny). So line passes through (1,2),(4,1). Does it also pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(2-1) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 not integer. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (1,2) and (4,2) is 0 (forbidden). So can't use.\n\n- Slope between (1,4) and (2,1) = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3. So line slope -3 passes through (1,4),(2,1). Does it also pass through (3,-2) not in T_5. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (1,4) and (2,4) = 0 (forbidden). So can't.\n\n- Slope between (1,4) and (4,1) = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1 (forbidden). So can't.\n\n- Slope between (1,4) and (4,2) = (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3 (sunny). Does it pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = 4 + (-2/3)*(2-1) = 4 - 2/3 = 10/3 not integer. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (2,1) and (2,4) = vertical line (forbidden). So can't.\n\n- Slope between (2,1) and (4,1) = 0 (forbidden). So can't.\n\n- Slope between (2,1) and (4,2) = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 (sunny). Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = 1 + (1/2)*(3-2) = 1.5 not integer. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (2,4) and (4,1) = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 (sunny). Does it pass through (3,? )? For x=3, y = 4 + (-3/2)*(3-2) = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5 not integer. So only two points.\n\n- Slope between (2,4) and (4,2) = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1 (forbidden). So can't.\n\n- Slope between (4,1) and (4,2) = vertical (forbidden). So can't.\n\nThus no line among remaining points contains three points. So we cannot cover the remaining 6 points with two sunny lines each covering 3 points. However, maybe we could use lines that cover fewer points but still cover all points with 5 lines? But we have capacity bound: each line can cover at most floor((k+1)/2) = floor(6/2) = 3 points. So each line can cover at most 3 points. If we have 5 lines, total capacity is at most 5*3 = 15, which matches total points. So we need each line to achieve the bound exactly (i.e., each line must contain exactly 3 points). If any line contains fewer than 3 points, then total covered points 15, leaving some uncovered. So each line must contain exactly 3 points. So we need 5 lines each covering exactly 3 points, disjoint. We found 3 lines covering 9 points. We need 2 more lines covering the remaining 6 points, each covering exactly 3 points. But we saw no line can cover 3 of the remaining points. So k=5 impossible.\n\nThus the solution's claim that only k=1 and k=3 are possible seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to check if there might be other configurations for odd k > 3 that use different sets of lines not necessarily achieving the bound for each line but still covering all points. However, the bound says each line can cover at most floor((k+1)/2) points. For odd k, floor((k+1)/2) = (k+1)/2. So each line can cover at most (k+1)/2 points. Since total points = k(k+1)/2, we need sum of points covered by k lines >= total points. The maximum sum is k * (k+1)/2 = total points. So we need each line to achieve the maximum bound exactly. So each line must contain exactly (k+1)/2 points. So each line must achieve the bound. The solution claims that only lines with |p+q|=2 achieve the bound. Let's verify that claim.\n\nWe derived earlier that for a sunny line, the number of points in T_k is at most floor((k+1)/2). The bound is derived from the fact that the sum x+y changes by |p+q| each step. The maximum number of points is floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1. For odd k, (k+1)/2 is integer. So we need floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 = (k+1)/2. Solve for |p+q|.\n\nLet m = (k+1)/2. Then we need floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 = m. So floor((k-1)/|p+q|) = m-1 = (k+1)/2 - 1 = (k-1)/2. So we need floor((k-1)/|p+q|) = (k-1)/2. Since (k-1)/2 is integer (k odd), we need (k-1)/|p+q| >= (k-1)/2 and (k-1)/2 + 1. So (k-1)/|p+q| >= (k-1)/2 => |p+q| <= 2. Also (k-1)/|p+q| (k-1)/2 + 1 => |p+q| > (k-1)/((k-1)/2 + 1) = (k-1) / ((k+1)/2) = 2(k-1)/(k+1). For k >= 3, this is less than 2. So |p+q| > something less than 2. So the only integer possibilities for |p+q| are 1 or 2. But we also need to consider the case |p+q| = 1. Let's examine if |p+q| = 1 can achieve the bound.\n\nIf |p+q| = 1, then floor((k-1)/1) + 1 = (k-1) + 1 = k. So the bound would be k, which is larger than (k+1)/2 for k > 1. However, the solution earlier argued that for |p+q| = 1, there is an additional restriction: q >= 2 (since p+q = 1 => p = 1 - q, p != 0 => q != 1). So q >= 2. Then the number of points is limited by floor(k/q) + 1 <= floor(k/2) + 1 = floor((k+2)/2). For odd k, floor(k/2) = (k-1)/2, so floor(k/2) + 1 = (k-1)/2 + 1 = (k+1)/2 = m. So indeed for |p+q| = 1, the bound can be exactly (k+1)/2 if q = 2 (or maybe q = 2 yields exactly m). Let's check: For odd k, say k = 2m-1? Actually k odd => k = 2m-1? Wait if k = 2m-1, then (k+1)/2 = m. For k odd, k = 2m-1 => m = (k+1)/2. Then floor(k/q) + 1 <= floor((2m-1)/q) + 1. For q = 2, floor((2m-1)/2) = m-1 (since (2m-1)/2 = m - 0.5). So floor = m-1. Then +1 = m. So bound = m = (k+1)/2. So indeed lines with |p+q| = 1 and q = 2 can achieve the bound. But does such a line exist? Let's examine p+q = 1 => p = 1 - q. If q = 2, then p = -1. So p = -1, q = 2 => p+q = 1. This corresponds to line -x - 2y = c? Actually p x - q y = c => -1 * x - 2 * y = c => -x - 2y = c => x + 2y = -c. So line x + 2y = constant. That's slope -1/2. Indeed we have that line. So lines with slope -1/2 (p = -1, q = 2) have |p+q| = 1. So they can achieve the bound. Similarly, p+q = -1 => p = -1 - q. For q = 2, p = -3, which gives p+q = -1? Actually p = -1 - q => p = -1 - 2 = -3, q = 2 => p+q = -1. That line is -3x - 2y = c => 3x + 2y = -c, slope -3/2? Wait slope = p/q? Actually line p x - q y = c => y = (p/q) x - c/q. So slope = p/q. For p = -3, q = 2 => slope = -3/2. That's not slope -1/2. So lines with p+q = -1 and q = 2 have slope -3/2. But does that achieve bound? Let's check. For p+q = -1, q = 2, p = -3. Then p+q = -1, |p+q| = 1. The bound from earlier for |p+q| = 1 case is floor(k/q) + 1 = floor(k/2) + 1 = (k+1)/2 for odd k. So lines with slope -3/2 also can achieve bound. But are they allowed? Let's check if they are sunny: slope -3/2 is not 0, infinite, or -1, so yes. So there might be lines with slope -3/2 that can achieve the bound. However, the solution's claim that only three possibilities (p,q) = (1,1), (-2,1), (-1,2) correspond to slopes 1, -2, -1/2. They omitted the possibility (p,q) = (-3,2) which yields slope -3/2. But does that line actually achieve the bound for T_k? Let's test for k=5. For slope -3/2, we need a line of form y = -3/2 x + b. Let's find b such that line contains 3 points in T_5. Use integer points: line equation 2y = -3x + 2b => 3x + 2y = 2b. So we need integer solutions to 3x + 2y = C (C even). Let's find C such that there are 3 integer solutions with x,y >=1 and x+y <=6.\n\nGeneral solution: For given C, integer solutions (x,y) satisfy 3x + 2y = C. Solve for y = (C - 3x)/2. So C - 3x must be even => parity condition: C and 3x have same parity. Since 3x parity = x parity (since 3 mod 2 = 1). So C parity = x parity. So x must have same parity as C.\n\nConstraints: x >= 1, y >= 1 => (C - 3x)/2 >= 1 => C - 3x >= 2 => x <= (C - 2)/3. Also x+y <=6 => x + (C - 3x)/2 <=6 => (2x + C - 3x)/2 <=6 => (C - x)/2 <=6 => C - x <=12 => x >= C - 12.\n\nThus x must satisfy max(1, C - 12) <= x <= floor((C - 2)/3). Also parity condition: x parity = C parity.\n\nNumber of integer x satisfying these constraints is the number of points on the line in T_5.\n\nWe need this number to be 3.\n\nLet's try to find C values.\n\nC must be even? Actually C = 2b, so C even. So C parity = 0 (even). So x must be even. So x must be even integer.\n\nNow constraints: x >= max(1, C - 12). Since x even, lower bound is the smallest even >= that.\n\nUpper bound: floor((C - 2)/3). Also x <= floor((C - 2)/3). Let's try C values.\n\nC must be such that there are at least 3 even x values in the interval [max(1, C-12), floor((C-2)/3)].\n\nLet's try C = 8 (even). Then lower bound = max(1, -4) = 1. Upper bound = floor((8-2)/3) = floor(6/3) = 2. So x in [1,2] even => x=2 only. So 1 point.\n\nC = 10: lower bound = max(1, -2) = 1. Upper bound = floor((10-2)/3) = floor(8/3) = 2. So x in [1,2] even => x=2 only. So 1 point.\n\nC = 12: lower bound = max(1, 0) = 1. Upper bound = floor((12-2)/3) = floor(10/3) = 3. So x in [1,3] even => x=2 only. So 1 point.\n\nC = 14: lower bound = max(1, 2) = 2. Upper bound = floor((14-2)/3) = floor(12/3) = 4. So x in [2,4] even => x=2,4 => 2 points.\n\nC = 16: lower bound = max(1, 4) = 4. Upper bound = floor((16-2)/3) = floor(14/3) = 4. So x in [4,4] even => x=4 => 1 point.\n\nC = 18: lower bound = max(1, 6) = 6. Upper bound = floor((18-2)/3) = floor(16/3) = 5. Lower bound > upper bound => no points.\n\nThus no C yields 3 points. So slope -3/2 lines cannot achieve bound for k=5. Let's test for general odd k: maybe slope -3/2 lines can achieve bound for some k? Let's analyze general case.\n\nGeneral bound for lines with |p+q| = 1: number of points <= floor(k/q) + 1. For p+q = -1, q > 0, p = -1 - q. So q >= 1. But p != 0 => -1 - q != 0 => q != -1, which is always true for q>0. Also p != -q => -1 - q != -q => -1 != 0, always true. So any q >= 1 yields a sunny line with p+q = -1. However, we also need gcd(p,q) = 1. For q = 2, p = -3, gcd(3,2) = 1, okay. For q = 3, p = -4, gcd(4,3) = 1, okay. So many possibilities.\n\nNow the bound floor(k/q) + 1. For odd k, we need floor(k/q) + 1 = (k+1)/2. Solve for q. Let k = 2m-1 (odd). Then (k+1)/2 = m. So we need floor((2m-1)/q) + 1 = m => floor((2m-1)/q) = m-1. So we need (2m-1)/q >= m-1 and m. So (2m-1)/q >= m-1 => q <= (2m-1)/(m-1) = (2m-1)/(m-1). For m >= 2 (k >= 3), (2m-1)/(m-1) = 2 + (1)/(m-1). So q <= 2 + 1/(m-1). Since q integer, q <= 2 for m >= 2. Also need (2m-1)/q m => q > (2m-1)/m = 2 - (1/m). So q > 2 - (1/m). For m >= 2, 2 - (1/m) is between 1.5 and 2. So q > something slightly less than 2, so q >= 2. So q must be exactly 2. So for odd k >= 3, the only q that works is q = 2. So indeed only q = 2 yields the bound. So lines with p+q = ±1 and q = 2 achieve the bound. That yields p = -1 (for p+q = 1) or p = -3 (for p+q = -1). So slopes are p/q = -1/2 or -3/2. Wait p+q = 1 => p = 1 - q = -1 (since q=2). So slope = p/q = -1/2. p+q = -1 => p = -1 - q = -3 (since q=2). So slope = -3/2. So both slopes -1/2 and -3/2 can achieve the bound. However, the solution only considered p = -1, q = 2 (slope -1/2) and p = 1, q = 1 (slope 1) and p = -2, q = 1 (slope -2). They omitted slope -3/2. But maybe slope -3/2 cannot achieve the bound for T_k because of other constraints (like the line must intersect T_k in exactly (k+1)/2 points, but maybe the parity condition prevents that for odd k?). Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe derived bound for |p+q| = 1 case: number of points <= floor(k/q) + 1. For q = 2, bound = floor(k/2) + 1 = (k-1)/2 + 1 = (k+1)/2. So bound is achievable if there exists a line with p+q = ±1, q = 2, that contains exactly (k+1)/2 points in T_k. The solution gave an example for p = -1, q = 2 (slope -1/2) that works for k = 3 (C=7). For k = 5, we found line x + 2y = 7 works (C=7). For k = 7, maybe line x + 2y = something works. Let's test general pattern: For T_k, we need line x + 2y = C to contain (k+1)/2 points. Let's find C such that number of integer solutions (x,y) in T_k is (k+1)/2.\n\nGeneral T_k: points (x,y) with x>=1, y>=1, x+y <= k+1. For line x + 2y = C, we need integer so